  "interpretation": "## What changed\n\nOver 90 days, 2,710 people used the tool and 64.53% came back more than once. Weekly users peaked at 830 in mid-August, fell to 323 in late September, then rose to 392. Yet estimated work kept climbing, from 185,182 to 632,495 a week. This suggests fewer, but busier, people.\n\nIn the last 14 days, users rose 15.63% while actions rose 90.77%. Actions growing faster than people may mean each person is doing more. People also moved to new releases quickly: 2.0.8 reached 56.28% of weekly users, and 2.3.9 already reached 27.59% in the newest partial week.\n\n## How people use it\n\nEveryday cell and table work makes up 63.87% of actions, mostly reading and writing values. \"Work\" here is an estimate, not time or speed: quick reads count 1, everyday edits count 3 and heavy data work counts 10. By that measure, macros are 7.94% of actions but 16.97% of work, and Power Query data imports are 3.93% of actions but 10.72% of work. Advanced use is real: most people who tried macros (77.23%) or Power Query (71.66%) did so on their first day.\n\nIn the last 30 days, a middle AI assistant session had 9 actions, but sessions of 201 or more actions were 4.81% of sessions and 48.45% of actions. 66.03% of sessions mixed areas, most often cells with worksheets. Of new people, 51.56% returned after a week and 39.83% after three weeks. Workdays averaged 25,430 actions versus 12,876 on weekends. Typical waits ranged from 0.07 seconds for cell work to 2.26 seconds for Power Query; waits depend on workbook size and computer, so they do not score quality.\n\nSince late September, AI assistant users (1,744) averaged 203.1 actions each, while command line users (145) averaged 538.9. These are different people over a shorter window, but command line users may lean more on cell work (80.44% of their actions).\n\n## Where people get it\n\nGitHub release files lead with 24,115 downloads, then VS Code installs (8,875), the npm AI assistant package (4,856) and the npm command line package (3,213). Weekly npm downloads grew from 2,891 to 5,178. Release 2.3.3 (577) and 2.3.9 (435) were the most downloaded recent releases. Over 4 days to October 8, GitHub and VS Code gained 1,006 downloads.\n\nDownloads count downloads, not people. One person can download several times, and updates and automated installs add to totals. Channels overlap, so totals should not be added. npm covers the last 12 months; others count since each channel started.\n\n## What we will improve\n\nThese are plans, not findings. We will make long sessions and heavy data work, like macros and Power Query, smoother. We will help new people find advanced features and keep returning after the first week.",
